module gunbc.instruments.text_at_free_monoid_run_carrier_witness

// WHAT THIS WITNESS HOLDS. A text (String) value is the free monoid over Char: std.coercion
// declares `String -> FreeMonoid` among the GROUNDED identity carriers (data
// grounded_primitive_coproduct_identities, operator Ruling 3 2026-07-15: "a String IS a
// FreeMonoid<Char> - strings over an alphabet are the free monoid"). So text inhabits a
// FreeMonoid carrier exactly when that carrier runs over Char, and a call that demands a run of
// anything else must refuse a text argument. Before this rule the pair carried NO verdict at all:
// measured at main, `list_append(["a"], "bc")` compiled with zero diagnostics-class refusals --
// only the counted advisory "declared-type inhabitance is UNMODELED at the direct call argument
// for parameter 'right'" -- and the claim run evaluated it to ["a", "bc"], because the interpreter's
// concat arm pushes whatever is not a carrier as one item (v1.compiler.interpreter). That is the
// list_push/list_append mix-up an author writes by hand, paid for by a reviewer.
//
// WHY THE GREEN ROWS ARE KEYED ON THE REFUSAL CLASS AND NOT ON "NO DIAGNOSTICS". The census row
// set is the WHOLE compile's, not the probed fixture's (gunbc.compile_census_probe
// blocking_class_census doc), and the algebra-spelled FreeMonoid position carries counted
// advisories of other classes on legitimate calls (std.algebra's own list_push 'item' position is
// one). A row keyed on gunbc.diagnostic.DeclaredTypeNotInhabited is the one that cannot go green
// by an unrelated cause: it is the class this refusal belongs to, and the green rows are the
// calls that rule must NOT fire on.
//
// WHY THE WALL READS ONLY THE POSITION IT IS JUDGING. The first attempt at this rule grounded the
// demanded element from ANY sibling argument, which refused a perfectly valid call: for
// `fn mixed<T, U>(a: FreeMonoid<T>, b: List<U>)` called `mixed(a: "ab", b: [1])`, the Int from b's
// U grounded a's position and a verdict was invented for a type variable that call never demanded
// (review 97406 finding 1; gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed -- every path succeeds or fails with a
// verdict it can name, and no plausible output is fabricated). The rule as landed reads the element
// from the position's OWN declared type, so control_two_element_variables below is the row that
// holds that boundary, and the refusal itself lives in the relation (declared_type_inhabitance)
// rather than in a second checker at the call seam (the ruling the seam's own comment records:
// tidy-ant-251, 2026-10-20 -- the call's own solved map, no second solver, one rule at the seam).

//
// WHY THIS MODULE IS AN INSTRUMENT AND NOT A CLAIM MODULE (moved out of `test.claim` on 2026-11-27).
// Its legs read census rows, and the census door REFUSES under the claim route for a measured, mechanical
// reason -- a second resident pool demanded over the corpus roots. The finding, with both verbatim causes
// and the logs that carry them, is stated once beside the door-choice comment of
// gunbc.instruments.list_append_element_argument_wall ("WHERE THE DOOR ANSWERS AND WHERE IT REFUSES"); it
// is cited here rather than copied (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority). A leg whose door refuses
// cannot fail on that route, so it is not evidence there; these legs are executed one per dispatch under
// `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v1 --source-root src/v2 --entry
// dag/gunbc/instruments/text_at_free_monoid_run_carrier_witness.dag --function <leg>`, where the observed
// arm answers, and each leg carries its own exit status: 0 held, 1 a finding, 2 could not run, cause named.

import gunbc.compile_census_probe {
  census_exit,
  census_verdict,
  census_sum_verdict,
  blocking_class_census,
  census_leg_wire,
  census_legs_wire
}
import gunbc.cli_wire { CliWireResponse }
// WHY THE KEY IS READ FROM THE AUTHORITY'S OUTPUT RATHER THAN FROM ITS RENDERER. The renderer of this
// refusal's subject is `v1.compiler.infer::inhabitance_refusal_position_label`, and a claim CANNOT import
// it -- that is a layer fact, not an oversight to code around. A claim lives in the WITNESS layer, whose
// roots are dag + src/v2 (data gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots::witness_layer_roots), while
// module v1.compiler.infer is declared under src/v1. Measured at this head with this head's own
// claim_batch, one binary, one tree: `--source-root dag --source-root src/v2` gives `error: unresolved
// import: module 'v1.compiler.infer' not found`, 0 rows, exit 1; `--source-root dag --source-root src/v1
// --source-root src/v2` resolves the entry and evaluates all 17 witnesses, then emits NOT ONE claim row
// and exits 2 -- three roots is the compile-only lane (the same extra root the compile-clean witness
// cites for its v1.std.core.Node / v2.std.node.Node collision), not a claims lane. Calling the renderer
// from here is therefore unavailable at exactly the layer where a claim row can exist.
//
// So the witness reads the row the renderer ALREADY PRODUCED: rows of the refusal's class, narrowed to
// those whose subject_name -- the compiler's own rendered text, never a copy of it held in this file --
// mentions the parameter its own fixture declares. The only literal this file supplies is that parameter
// name, which is the fixture's, not the label's. That is the keyed form the census annotation calls
// exact (class AND subject_name), and it cannot go stale if the label's prose is reworded, because no
// prose of the label is stored here to go stale. Where a fixture calls POSITIONALLY (the repro calls
// `list_append(["a"], "bc")`, exactly as the ticket spells it), the name keyed is the one the CALLEE
// declares at that position -- std.algebra declares `fn list_append<T>(left: FreeMonoid<T>,
// right: FreeMonoid<T>)`, so the second argument's parameter is 'right' -- which makes such a row a
// claim about the positional mapping as well as about the refusal, and it is judged by the same
// compiler that renders the label, not by anything this file asserts.
import gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census { CompileDiagnosticCensus, CensusObserved, CensusNotRunnable }
import std.algebra { string_contains, string_concat_join }
import std.process { ProcessExit }
import std.types { Bool }

// The repro, as the ticket states it: a text actual at the second FreeMonoid<T> parameter of
// std.algebra list_append, whose first argument grounds the element to String.
data repro_list_append_text_actual: String = "module witness_fm_repro\nimport std.algebra { list_append }\nimport std.list { list_cover }\nimport std.types { String, Bool, List }\nfn eq_str(a: String, b: String) -> Bool { a == b }\nfn probe() -> Bool { list_cover(held: list_append([\"a\"], \"bc\"), expected: [\"a\", \"bc\"], eq: eq_str) }\n"

// The same call over two runs of the same element: what the author who writes the repro usually
// means, and the row that must stay refused-by-nothing. POSITIONAL, like the repro above it, so the
// three subjects in this family (this one, the repro, and `control_list_push_element` below) differ
// ONLY at the one actual -- a labelled twin would put a second difference in the experiment (call
// syntax) and let a refusal be attributed to the spelling rather than to the argument. The row filter
// still keys on the compiler's own subject text (`'right'`, `'item'`), which comes from the formal's
// parameter identity and not from how the call was written.
data control_list_append_two_runs: String = "module witness_fm_two_runs\nimport std.algebra { list_append }\nimport std.types { String, List }\nfn probe() -> List<String> { list_append([\"a\"], [\"b\"]) }\n"

// list_push is the OTHER operation: an element where an element is required. The rule must not
// touch it; its carrier position is the same FreeMonoid spelling, its element position is a bare
// type parameter.
data control_list_push_element: String = "module witness_fm_list_push\nimport std.algebra { list_push }\nimport std.types { String, List }\nfn probe() -> List<String> { list_push([\"a\"], \"b\") }\n"

// The identity std.coercion actually declares: text is a run over Char, so a call whose demanded
// element IS Char takes text at a run position.
data control_text_at_char_run: String = "module witness_fm_char_run\nimport std.algebra { list_append }\nimport std.types { String, Char }\nfn probe() -> String { list_append(left: \"ab\", right: \"bc\") }\n"

data control_text_at_declared_char_run: String = "module witness_fm_declared_char_run\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String, Char }\nfn take(xs: FreeMonoid<Char>) -> Int { xs.count() }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: \"bc\") }\n"

// The same refusal with no generics in sight: a concrete FreeMonoid<String> parameter handed text.
data control_text_at_string_run: String = "module witness_fm_string_run\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String }\nfn take(xs: FreeMonoid<String>) -> Int { xs.count() }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: \"bc\") }\n"

// Unchanged refusals, in the class this witness keys on: a non-text scalar at a FreeMonoid carrier,
// and text at the container-alias (List) spelling.
data control_text_at_run_of_int: String = "module witness_fm_text_at_run_of_int\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String, Int }\nfn take(xs: FreeMonoid<Int>) -> Int { xs.count() }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: \"bc\") }\n"

// A non-text scalar at the carrier: refused before this rule and after it, by the arms that
// compare a kernel scalar actual against a structured declared head.
data control_int_at_run_carrier: String = "module witness_fm_int_at_run\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { Int, String }\nfn take<T>(xs: FreeMonoid<T>) -> Int { 1 }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: 5) }\n"

data control_text_at_list_alias: String = "module witness_fm_list_alias\nimport std.types { String, List }\nfn take(xs: List<String>) -> Int { 1 }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: \"bc\") }\n"

// Runs of two different elements at one element variable: the container spelling already refuses
// this; the row is here so the wall's class count is visible on both spellings.
data control_list_alias_two_run_elements: String = "module witness_fm_alias_two\nimport std.types { String, Int, List }\nfn take<T>(left: List<T>, right: List<T>) -> Int { 1 }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(left: [\"a\"], right: [1]) }\n"

// Two DIFFERENT element variables, one of them a run carrier handed text and the other a List handed
// a list of Int. This is a valid call (the text is a run over Char; b grounds its own U). A rule
// that borrowed an element from a sibling would refuse it; the rule here does not, because each
// position is judged against its own declared type.
// THE LEAF NAME DECIDES NOTHING (review 97588; gunbc.design_argument::closed_grounded_substrate).
// A LOCAL declaration spelled Char is not the declared Char the identity names, so text at a run
// over it is the same error as text at a run of Int -- and the spelling-first version of this rule
// admitted it. The row that keeps the two apart: the same text at the QUALIFIED declared Char is the
// identity and stays unrefused.
// DOES THE CALL ITSELF GROUND THE ELEMENT? The refusal may only read what the position demands, so
// this row is the one that proves the grounding happens in the call's own unification: an Int at a
// type parameter that a sibling run of String settles must refuse exactly as it does at the concrete
// `FreeMonoid<String>` above. On main the generic position says nothing at all.
data control_int_at_element_settled_by_sibling: String = "module witness_fm_sibling_ground\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String, Int }\nfn two<T>(left: FreeMonoid<T>, right: FreeMonoid<T>) -> Int { left.count() }\nfn probe() -> Int { two(left: [\"a\"], right: 1) }\n"

// A MODULE-LOCAL ALIAS OF THE CARRIER IS THE CARRIER: identity is settled to the declaration, so the
// alias spelling cannot be used to slip a text actual past the rule.
data control_text_at_local_alias_of_carrier: String = "module witness_fm_carrier_alias\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String }\ntype Run<e> = FreeMonoid<e>\nfn take(xs: Run<String>) -> Int { xs.count() }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: \"bc\") }\n"

data control_text_at_local_char_spelling_run: String = "module witness_fm_local_char\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String }\ntype Char { code: Int }\nfn take(xs: FreeMonoid<Char>) -> Int { xs.count() }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: \"bc\") }\n"

data control_text_at_qualified_char_run: String = "module witness_fm_qualified_char\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String }\nfn take(xs: FreeMonoid<std.types.Char>) -> Int { xs.count() }\nfn probe() -> Int { take(xs: \"bc\") }\n"

data control_two_element_variables: String = "module witness_fm_two_vars\nimport std.algebra { FreeMonoid }\nimport std.types { String, Int, List }\nfn mixed<T, U>(a: FreeMonoid<T>, b: List<U>) -> Int { 1 }\nfn probe() -> Int { mixed(a: \"ab\", b: [1]) }\n"

// THE ROWS THIS WALL'S REFUSAL CARRIES, narrowed by the parameter the fixture itself declares. The
// census row set is the WHOLE compile's, including every imported module (see the header of this file
// and #gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census::CompileDiagnosticCensusRow), so a count scoped to the class alone
// is the form that annotation calls INEXACT: main already emits DeclaredTypeNotInhabited rows of its own
// at other parameters of these very fixtures, and a class-only count can read one of those as this rule's
// verdict. Scoping to subject_name is the exact form -- and the subject_name compared here is the
// compiler's rendered text, filtered by containment of the parameter name this file declares, so this
// file stores none of the label's wording. A row whose count is 0 on a licensed twin is part of the key,
// not decoration: it is what stops main's unrelated refusal from satisfying the row.
fn wall_rows_at_parameter(source: String, parameter: String) -> CompileDiagnosticCensus {
  match blocking_class_census(source: source, wanted_class: "DeclaredTypeNotInhabited") {
    CensusObserved { rows: rows } => CensusObserved { rows: rows |> filter(r => string_contains(s: r.subject_name, pattern: string_concat_join(fields: ["'", parameter, "'"], separator: ""))) }
    CensusNotRunnable { cause: cause } => CensusNotRunnable { cause: cause }
  }
}

fn text_actual_at_run_of_string_is_refused_naming_the_right_parameter() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: repro_list_append_text_actual, parameter: "right"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

fn the_same_call_over_two_runs_is_not_refused() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_list_append_two_runs, parameter: "right"), holds: fn(n) { n == 0 }))
}

fn list_push_takes_an_element_where_an_element_is_required() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_list_push_element, parameter: "item"), holds: fn(n) { n == 0 }))
}

// Keyed on BOTH parameters the fixture actually names -- list_append declares (left, right), so
// filtering on an "xs" this call never spells would be green whatever the compiler did. Text over
// the declared Char is the identity std.types declares, so neither run position may be refused.
fn text_at_a_run_over_char_is_the_declared_identity() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_sum_verdict(first: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_char_run, parameter: "left"), second: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_char_run, parameter: "right"), holds: fn(n) { n == 0 }))
}

fn text_at_a_declared_run_over_char_is_not_refused() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_declared_char_run, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n == 0 }))
}

fn text_at_a_declared_run_of_string_is_refused_without_generics() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_string_run, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

// A TEXT AT A RUN-OF-INT, decided by the ruling this node carries and stated in ONE place: the data line
// below. The declared element is `Int` while the actual is a run of the declared Char, so this is the one
// fixture whose verdict belongs to the ruling rather than to the wall's arithmetic. Reading a run of Char
// as a run of code-point Ints CHANGES INTERPRETATION, and gunbc.design_argument::closed_grounded_substrate
// refuses the implicit coercion that would let it through -- "Bool does not implicitly inhabit Int" -- so
// the wall asks no brand authority whether Char may widen to the declared element, and REFUSES. The
// expected count is therefore 1 row at this wall's subject. Were the implicit licensing ever restored --
// the reading this node removes -- the same row would read 0 and ONLY that data line changes: the
// comparison reads the constant instead of hard-coding an oracle, so one file serves either reading.
// The constant is not a knob. The head binary is the arbiter of the count, a mismatch is a finding about
// the arm to be repaired in the arm, and this comparison is never re-keyed until it passes.
//
// What the ruling does NOT touch stays asserted: text at a run over the declared Char is std.coercion's
// grounded identity (text_at_a_run_over_char_is_the_declared_identity), and a non-text scalar at a carrier
// -- `take(xs: 5)` -- is refused by the arms above this wall, before it and after. Under main's binary
// this fixture carries no inhabitance class at all: 0 rows of DeclaredTypeNotInhabited,
// DeclaredTypeInhabitanceUndecided and DeclaredTypeInhabitanceUnmodeled. That is what makes this row
// DISCRIMINATING rather than a positive control -- RED on main, GREEN on the head (the s17 map leg carries
// the rc).
data expected_rows_at_text_over_a_declared_int_run: Int = 1

fn text_at_a_declared_run_of_int_is_refused_as_a_change_of_interpretation() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_run_of_int, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n == expected_rows_at_text_over_a_declared_int_run }))
}

fn an_int_at_a_run_carrier_is_refused_as_it_already_was() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: blocking_class_census(source: control_int_at_run_carrier, wanted_class: "DeclaredTypeNotInhabited"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

fn text_at_the_list_alias_spelling_is_still_refused_once() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_list_alias, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n == 1 }))
}

fn runs_of_different_elements_at_one_element_variable_are_refused() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: blocking_class_census(source: control_list_alias_two_run_elements, wanted_class: "DeclaredTypeNotInhabited"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

fn a_sibling_at_a_different_element_variable_grounds_nothing_at_the_run_carrier() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_two_element_variables, parameter: "a"), holds: fn(n) { n == 0 }))
}


fn a_local_char_spelling_at_a_run_carrier_is_refused() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_local_char_spelling_run, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

fn the_qualified_char_declaration_at_a_run_carrier_is_the_identity() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_qualified_char_run, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n == 0 }))
}

fn an_int_at_an_element_the_sibling_run_grounds_is_refused() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: blocking_class_census(source: control_int_at_element_settled_by_sibling, wanted_class: "DeclaredTypeNotInhabited"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

fn text_at_a_module_local_alias_of_the_run_carrier_is_refused() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_local_alias_of_carrier, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

// THE STANDARD LIBRARY ASKS FOR THIS PAIR, MEASURED. src/v2/std/text.dag hands a String at a
// generic run position with nothing to settle its element: `is_empty(xs: s)` (:25, :34, :59, :118),
// `for_all(xs: s, predicate: fn(c) { ... })` (:191, :195, :200), `list_append(left: acc, right: field)`
// over two Strings (:165). The first version of this rule refused all eleven sites -- the heal round
// of PR #16894 reported DeclaredTypeNotInhabited [RefusedTextAtRunOfNonCharElement] at
// src/v2/std/text.dag:25:16, :28:23, :34:17, :59:9, :116:27, :118:48, :165:49, :165:61, :191:16,
// :195:15, :200:17, each declared 'Node(FreeMonoid<Primitive()>)' -- an unsolved element read as
// "some declaration that is not Char". These two rows are the corpus, held as controls: an element
// this call does not settle is no verdict, and the rule that forgets it refuses the standard library.
data control_text_at_run_element_unsettled: String = "module witness_fm_unsettle\nimport std.types { String, Bool, List }\nfn is_empty<T>(xs: List<T>) -> Bool { true }\nfn probe() -> Bool { is_empty(xs: \"abc\") }\n"

data control_text_at_run_element_settled_by_a_lambda: String = "module witness_fm_lambda_settle\nimport std.types { String, Bool, List }\nfn for_all<T>(xs: List<T>, predicate: fn(T) -> Bool) -> Bool { true }\nfn probe() -> Bool { for_all(xs: \"abc\", predicate: fn(c) { c == \"a\" }) }\n"

// ABSTAIN, with the reason named: this fixture's element never settles from any declaration -- it
// could only come from a sibling argument or the author's intent, neither of which is a witness --
// so the new rule refuses to ground it (unsettled element means Unidentified, never a refusal
// borrowed from a sibling) and states nothing about whether the call is well typed. The row records
// what the PRE-EXISTING arm does here, observed as 1 on the main binary at 56d460116d6252f6ec8dfe889ea358c804685861;
// it is not a claim that this call is ill typed, and it is not re-keyed to agree with the wall.
fn text_at_a_run_whose_element_the_call_never_settles_is_no_verdict() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_run_element_unsettled, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

// ABSTAIN, with the reason named, as above: a lambda parameter settles the element only through an
// inference the wall does not read as a declaration, so the rule states nothing here. Observed as 1
// on the main binary at the same sha: pre-existing behaviour, recorded not adjudicated.
fn text_at_a_run_whose_element_a_lambda_settles_is_no_verdict() -> ProcessExit {
  census_exit(census_verdict(census: wall_rows_at_parameter(source: control_text_at_run_element_settled_by_a_lambda, parameter: "xs"), holds: fn(n) { n >= 1 }))
}

// THE ENROLLED ADDRESSES FOR THESE LEGS, and the same rule as its sibling
// #gunbc.instruments.list_append_element_argument_wall::wall_all_legs_wire: a module nothing imports is
// dangling (gunbc.design_argument::consumption), so each of the node's three carrier controls gets its
// own `gunbc test` label and the full seventeen-leg population gets one fold. The rows live in
// #gunbc.instrument_targets as `CliWireProgramProducer` bindings naming this module and these functions;
// the host evaluates them WET over the witness layer roots named in the header above, and maps the
// leg's ProcessExit through src/v1/stage0/src/target_invocation_host.rs -- 0 held, 1 a finding, 2 the
// door did not answer.

fn carrier_leg_identity(name: String) -> String {
  concat("text-at-free-monoid-run-carrier-witness/", name)
}

// THE DISCRIMINATING RED of the carrier: a text actual at a `run` whose element is settled and not
// Char is refused, and the refusal names the parameter. rc 1 without the wall, rc 0 with it.
// `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:free-monoid-carrier-text-at-string-run-refused`.
fn text_at_string_run_refused_wire() -> CliWireResponse {
  census_leg_wire(
    identity: carrier_leg_identity(name: "text_actual_at_run_of_string_is_refused_naming_the_right_parameter"),
    verdict: text_actual_at_run_of_string_is_refused_naming_the_right_parameter()
  )
}

// POSITIVE CONTROL: the same call over two runs is not refused -- the wall must not fire where the
// arguments already inhabit the declared carrier.
// `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:free-monoid-carrier-two-runs-admitted`.
fn two_runs_admitted_wire() -> CliWireResponse {
  census_leg_wire(
    identity: carrier_leg_identity(name: "the_same_call_over_two_runs_is_not_refused"),
    verdict: the_same_call_over_two_runs_is_not_refused()
  )
}

// POSITIVE CONTROL: the mix-up this class exists to catch stays lawful where it is lawful --
// `list_push` takes an element where an element is required.
// `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:free-monoid-carrier-list-push-admitted`.
fn list_push_admitted_wire() -> CliWireResponse {
  census_leg_wire(
    identity: carrier_leg_identity(name: "list_push_takes_an_element_where_an_element_is_required"),
    verdict: list_push_takes_an_element_where_an_element_is_required()
  )
}

// THE WHOLE CARRIER IN ONE ADDRESS -- all seventeen legs, each row printed with its own verdict and
// code, the run's exit the worst of them (#gunbc.compile_census_probe::census_worst_exit). Any leg
// added to this module later must be added here, which is what keeps "a leg nothing runs" from
// recurring after review 99292. `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:free-monoid-carrier-all-legs`.
fn carrier_all_legs_wire() -> CliWireResponse {
  census_legs_wire([
    text_at_string_run_refused_wire(),
    two_runs_admitted_wire(),
    list_push_admitted_wire(),
